---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import legacyPages from '../../data/legacy-pages.json';
const page = legacyPages['blogs-offer-questions'];
---

<SiteLayout {...page} legacy={true} noindex={false}>
  <main id="main" class="article-content">
    <nav class="article-breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/blogs/"
        >Hiring &amp; career guides</a
      >
    </nav><p class="overline">Quantum Leaps · Practical guidance</p><h1>
      Four Questions Before Accepting a Role
    </h1><p class="article-meta">
      By Quantum Leaps · Published and reviewed 25 October 2026 · 2 min read
    </p><p class="article-note">
      Clarify ownership, expectations and support before making your next move.
    </p><h2>1. What does success look like?</h2><p>
      Ask what the team needs in the first three months and what can
      realistically wait. Look for agreement between the hiring manager and
      other interviewers. A title can hide very different expectations.
    </p><h2>2. What will I own?</h2><p>
      Clarify the reporting line, decision rights, team resources and budget.
      Ask which decisions you can make independently and where approvals are
      needed. Discuss the boundaries between your role and neighbouring
      functions.
    </p><h2>3. Why is the role open?</h2><p>
      A new role, a replacement and a team reorganisation each bring different
      questions. Ask what has changed and what the organisation has learned. You
      do not need another employee’s private details to understand the operating
      context.
    </p><h2>4. What support will be available?</h2><p>
      Discuss manager access, onboarding, feedback and access to tools or
      people. Confirm location and working arrangements in the written offer
      rather than relying on a general careers statement.
    </p><p>
      Use the answers to decide which tradeoffs suit your goals. Ask for
      clarification where a written offer differs from the conversations.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/contact/">Discuss a hiring brief →</a> · <a href="/submit-cv/"
        >Share your CV →</a
      >
    </p>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
